// ============================================================================
== 5.1 Adjectives and Adverbs

An adjective describes a noun; an adverb describes everything else. The paper tests the choice in the same three positions every year: after a linking verb, after an action verb, and inside a comparison.

#rule-box("Where each one goes")[
  + *After a linking verb* — be, seem, become, look, feel, taste, smell, sound, appear — the word is an adjective: the soup *tastes good*, she *looks happy*, he *became angry*.
  + *After an action verb* the word is an adverb: he ran *quickly*, she spoke *softly*, they worked *hard*.
  + *Before a noun* the word is an adjective: a *careful* driver, an *early* train.
  + *Before another adjective or adverb* the word is an adverb: *very* tall, *quite* quickly, *extremely* well.
]

#trap-box("Hard, hardly; late, lately; near, nearly")[
  These four pairs are not comparative forms of each other. *Hard* means with effort, *hardly* means almost not. *Late* means after time, *lately* means recently. *Near* means close, *nearly* means almost. *High* means far up, *highly* means very much. The paper uses all four pairs, and the -ly form is never simply the adverb of the plain one.
]

#worked-example(
  1,
  "The linking verb takes an adjective",
  [“The soup tastes \_\_\_ and the children ate it \_\_\_.” Options: good / quickly, well / quick, good / quick, well / quickly.],
  [
    *Step 1 — the first blank.* *Tastes* is a linking verb here: it connects the soup to a description of the soup, so the word is an adjective. *Good* is the adjective; *well* is the adverb.

    *Step 2 — the second blank.* *Ate* is an action verb, and the blank describes how the children ate, so the word is an adverb: *quickly*.

    *Step 3 — eliminate.* Any option with *well* in the first blank or *quick* in the second is wrong. Only *good / quickly* survives.

    *Check.* Substitute *seems* for *tastes*, which is also a linking verb: “the soup seems good” works, “the soup seems well” does not.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

// ============================================================================
== 5.2 Comparatives and Superlatives

A comparative compares two things; a superlative compares three or more. The form depends on the length of the adjective, and the irregulars have to be learned.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch05_comparatives.svg",
  "Positive, comparative and superlative, with the four irregulars. The rule column is what decides the form; the irregulars are the four the paper reuses.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("Three rules and three traps")[
  + *One syllable* takes *-er* and *-est*: tall, taller, tallest.
  + *Two syllables ending in -y* change the *y* to *i*: happy, happier, happiest.
  + *Two syllables or more* take *more* and *most*: careful, more careful, most careful.
  + *Never double the comparison*: more better, most tallest and more easier are all wrong.
  + *Two things take the comparative*, not the superlative: the *better* of the two, not the best of the two.
  + *Than* follows a comparative; *in* or *of* follows a superlative: taller *than* his brother, the tallest *in* the class.
]

#worked-example(
  2,
  "The better of the two",
  [“Between the two proposals, this one is clearly \_\_\_ and it will be \_\_\_ to implement.” Options: the best / easier, better / easier, the better / more easy, best / easiest],
  [
    *Step 1 — count the things being compared.* There are two proposals, so the comparative is required: *better*, not *best*.

    *Step 2 — decide the article.* A comparative used as a noun phrase takes *the*: *the better* of the two. Without *the*, the sentence still works, but the option list pairs each first choice with a second, so decide the second first.

    *Step 3 — check the second blank.* *Easy* is a two-syllable adjective ending in *-y*, so the comparative is *easier*, not *more easy*.

    *Check.* Both forms follow the rule for their adjective, and both are comparatives because only two things are in play. The pair is *the better / easier*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

// ============================================================================
== 5.3 Misplaced and Dangling Modifiers

A modifier belongs next to the word it describes. Move it, and the sentence says something the writer did not intend — which is exactly what the paper asks you to notice.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch05_modifiers.svg",
  "Three modifier faults and the repair each one needs. All three are tested by asking which word the modifier is describing.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("Three faults, three repairs")[
  + *Dangling:* the modifier has nothing to describe. “Walking to school, the rain began” — the rain was not walking. *Repair:* name the doer — “Walking to school, I was caught in the rain.”
  + *Misplaced:* the modifier describes the wrong word. “She served tea to the guests in paper cups” — the guests are not in cups. *Repair:* move it — “She served tea in paper cups to the guests.”
  + *Squinting:* the modifier could describe either of two words. “What he said clearly annoyed her” — did he speak clearly, or was she clearly annoyed? *Repair:* move it to the side you mean.
]

#worked-example(
  3,
  "Repairing a dangling modifier",
  [Which sentence is correct? (a) Having finished the assignment, the television was switched on. (b) Having finished the assignment, she switched on the television. (c) Having finished the assignment, it was time for television. (d) Having finished the assignment, television was watched by her.],
  [
    *Step 1 — find the modifier.* *Having finished the assignment* is a participial phrase, and the doer of *having finished* must be the subject of the main clause.

    *Step 2 — test each subject.* (a) makes the television finish the assignment. (c) makes *it* finish it. (d) makes the television finish it again. Only (b) gives a subject — *she* — who could have finished an assignment.

    *Step 3 — confirm.* The repaired sentence reads “Having finished the assignment, she switched on the television”, and the doer of both actions is the same person.

    *Check.* Ask who performed the action in the opening phrase. If the answer is not the subject of the main clause, the modifier is dangling.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#trap-box("Only, even, almost, just")[
  These four adverbs change the meaning according to where they sit. *Only Chidi passed* means one person passed; *Chidi only passed* means he did not excel. The paper tests *only* more than the other three, and the rule is always the same: put it immediately before the word it limits.
]

// ============================================================================
== 5.4 Prepositions and Their Pairs

The prepositions of time and place are decided by a single sentence — *at a point, on a surface or day, in an enclosure or period* — but the fixed pairs have to be learned verb by verb.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch05_prepositions.svg",
  "Prepositions grouped by the question they answer, with the fixed pairs in the last row. The fixed pairs are the ones a dictionary will not help you with.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("The pairs worth memorising")[
  + *depend on, insist on, rely on, concentrate on* — never *depend of* or *insist to*.
  + *consist of, approve of, be capable of, be afraid of, be tired of*.
  + *comply with, deal with, agree with* (a person) but *agree to* (a proposal).
  + *prefer to, married to, similar to, opposed to, according to*.
  + *interested in, involved in, succeed in, believe in, arrive in* (a city) but *arrive at* (a place).
]

#worked-example(
  4,
  "At, on or in",
  [“The lecture will begin \_\_\_ 9 a.m. \_\_\_ Monday \_\_\_ the main hall.” Options: at / on / in, on / at / in, in / on / at, at / in / on],
  [
    *Step 1 — the time.* *9 a.m.* is a point in time, so the preposition is *at*.

    *Step 2 — the day.* *Monday* is a day, and days take *on*.

    *Step 3 — the place.* *The main hall* is an enclosed space, so the preposition is *in*.

    *Check.* Apply the one-sentence rule to each blank: point takes *at*, day takes *on*, enclosure takes *in*. The answer is *at / on / in*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

// ============================================================================
== 5.5 Connectives and Register

A connective is chosen by the job the sentence needs: addition, contrast, cause, result, purpose or sequence. Choosing by length or by formality is how candidates lose the mark.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch05_connectors.svg",
  "Connectives grouped by the job the sentence needs, with the punctuation each one demands. The punctuation column is where most of the marks are lost.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("Connective or adverb?")[
  + *Conjunctions* join clauses inside one sentence: and, but, because, although, since, unless, so.
  + *Conjunctive adverbs* join sentences and take a semicolon or a full stop before them: however, therefore, moreover, nevertheless, consequently.
  + *A comma plus however is a comma splice.* “The road was flooded, however we arrived early” is wrong; “The road was flooded; however, we arrived early” is right.
  + *Because* may not follow a comma alone: “He passed, because he studied” is acceptable in modern usage but the paper prefers no comma before a final *because*.
]

#worked-example(
  5,
  "Choosing the connective by its job",
  [“The team trained for months \_\_\_ it lost the final match \_\_\_ the players were exhausted.” Options: and / because, but / because, because / so, so / although],
  [
    *Step 1 — the first blank.* The sentence sets up an expectation — months of training — and then defeats it with a loss. That is contrast, so the connective is *but*.

    *Step 2 — the second blank.* The exhaustion is the reason for the loss, so the connective is *because*.

    *Step 3 — eliminate.* *And* would add a second fact with no contrast; *so* would make the loss the result of the training; *although* would reverse the cause and make exhaustion the thing that was true despite the loss.

    *Check.* Read the connective back as its plain meaning: “The team trained for months *however* it lost the final match *for the reason that* the players were exhausted.” Both substitutions preserve the sense, so *but / because* is the pair.
  ],
  tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
)

